{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778196621,"nonce":"IIsCiuMI04NG5aPKROXs61+W8sY=","message":"**Linearization machinery — generality and limits** (per user-asked clarification).\n\nThe mechanism is `jax.jacobian` (or `jax.jvp`/`jax.vjp`) over a forward step that takes the augmented closed-loop state and produces `(next_state, outputs)`. Not specific to any plant or model architecture.\n\n**Works generally for:**\n- Any feedbax `Graph` whose `step` method is jit/autodiff-compatible (the design contract of feedbax components).\n- Multi-layer networks, hierarchical controllers, multiple feedback loops — all just deeper computation graphs from JAX's perspective.\n- Custom plants (point-mass, two-link arm, MJX MuJoCo bodies *with caveats — see below*) as long as their dynamics are JAX-traceable.\n- Arbitrary cycle-wire topologies — `Graph.step`'s cycle-port threading puts them into the augmented state, JAX differentiates through.\n\n**Hard limits (where it stops working or degrades):**\n\n1. **Non-differentiable ops in the forward step.** Hard thresholds, `jnp.argmax`/`argmin` for control flow, integer-typed state used in arithmetic, data-dependent `lax.cond`/`lax.while`. JAX autodiff returns zero gradients (or NaNs). Workaround: smooth approximations (sigmoid for thresholds, softmax for argmax). Common in RL policies; rarely in feedbax-style continuous controllers.\n\n2. **Non-reparameterizable stochasticity.** Discrete-categorical sampling, etc. — linearization at any specific noise instance won't capture the stochastic effect. Sidestep by linearizing around a *deterministic* nominal trajectory (noise zeroed) and treating stochastic sensitivity as the disturbance channel `w`.\n\n3. **Non-smooth dynamics.** Rigid-body contact transitions in MuJoCo are piecewise-smooth — linearization is valid only within a single contact regime. Practical implication: short-horizon linearization is fine; long-horizon may straddle regimes.\n\n4. **State dimension.** Augmented-state size N → N×N Jacobians per timestep. Memory scales `O(N²·T)` for horizon-T LTV. ~393-dim controller for Part 2.5 → ~150k entries × T (fine). 10k-dim transformer policy → 100M × T (memory-intensive but possible). Power iteration on the LTV operator avoids materializing the Toeplitz lift, so N² scaling matters mostly per-step.\n\n5. **Local-only validity.** Exact only at the operating point; error grows nonlinearly far from it. Intrinsic to linearization, not a JAX limitation. The induced-gain analyser sidesteps this by computing *closed-loop* gain (controller keeps state near nominal); but if adversarial `w` drives state far, linear gain underestimates true behaviour.\n\n**Practical conclusion.** For feedbax models built from standard components (RNNs, channels, mechanics, intervenors), linearization generalises cleanly. For anything with hard discontinuities, contact dynamics, or discrete sampling at the dynamics level, smooth those out or accept that linearization captures only one regime / one realisation.","files":null}]}